WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.150 --> 00:00:07.850
It Comes At Night is probably my favorite
horror movie of the year so far because of

00:00:07.850 --> 00:00:10.720
the psychological quandaries the film poses.

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:12.480
Questions like, Who opened the door?

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:13.480
What is “it”?

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:16.219
Is the ending of the movie dream or reality?

00:00:16.219 --> 00:00:21.320
In this video, I’ll be giving my interpretations
on some of the many ambiguities posed by writer/director

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:25.010
Trey Edward Shults, who I’ll be referring
to as Shultsy for the rest of this video for

00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:26.929
some reason.

00:00:26.929 --> 00:00:29.880
Just for clarity's sake, let’s do a quick
recap of all the character names.

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:32.870
The original family is led by Paul and his
wife is Sarah.

00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:34.790
They have a teenage son is named Travis.

00:00:34.790 --> 00:00:38.550
The guest family consists of the father, Will,
who is married to Kim and they have a young

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:40.110
boy named Andrew.

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:43.880
There will be spoilers ahead, so if you haven’t
seen this movie, just pause this video, spend

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:47.270
the next 97 minutes watching it, then unpause
this video.

00:00:47.270 --> 00:00:48.270
Ready?

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:49.270
Go.

00:00:49.270 --> 00:00:50.310
Who Opened The Door?

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:53.560
The end of the film is driven by the introduction
of the sickness into the household.

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:57.940
This starts one night when Travis is awake,
as he often is, to find the little boy Andrew

00:00:57.940 --> 00:00:59.900
sleeping in the middle of the living room.

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:03.560
He wakes him up and returns him to his room,
where he crawls into bed with his parents,

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:04.860
who don’t seem to wake up.

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:07.920
As Travis is returning to bed he hears something
in the house.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:11.970
He approaches the door and finds that it’s
partially open, with their dog, who had run

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:14.250
away earlier dying on the floor.

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:17.370
This of course causes panic and finger pointing
from both families.

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:21.010
Paul worries that Andrew wandered out and
caught the disease from the dog, and he uses

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:25.220
the fact that Andrew was out of his room as
evidence that he may be a sleepwalker.

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:29.250
Will points out the fact that Andrew can barely
reach the doorhandles then implies that Travis

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:31.600
may have actually been the one to open the
door.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:35.670
The obvious flaw in that story is that we
know Andrew was out of his room, so unless

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:39.100
someone opened it for him, it’s proof he’s
able to reach the doorknob.

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:41.909
But the possibility of it being Travis still
exists.

00:01:41.909 --> 00:01:45.750
Kim knows he has sleep issues and tends to
wander around after dark due to their prior

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:48.110
kitchen conversation in the middle of the
night.

00:01:48.110 --> 00:01:50.460
Kim and Will both seem to be fairly innocent.

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:53.850
We can see them sleeping while when Travis
brings Andrew back to their room.

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:58.400
It’s easy to assume that Paul and Sarah
would also be asleep at the time, and that

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:01.690
they would have no reason to open the door
and endanger themselves like that.

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:05.220
After thinking about it further, I don’t
think Sarah could be responsible because of

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:07.210
her generally non-malicious attitude.

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:11.180
I can, however, come up with a reason that
Paul might have been the one to open it.

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:14.489
Paul is very hostile towards Will as soon
as they meet.

00:02:14.489 --> 00:02:19.450
The only reason, it would, that he even allows
Will to survive at first is that Will mentions

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:22.719
the fact that he and his family have food
but no water.

00:02:22.719 --> 00:02:27.329
Paul formulates the plan to have them move
in, use them to learn how to be more self-sustainable,

00:02:27.329 --> 00:02:31.489
as we see in the scenes with Travis learning
from Will; before framing them and killing

00:02:31.489 --> 00:02:36.670
them in order to gain sole access to the livestock
while maintaining trust of his own family.

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:41.349
If that is his plan, it is a most devious
one.

00:02:41.349 --> 00:02:45.689
Some might go so far as to say that he lured
Will in, using the fire set at the beginning

00:02:45.689 --> 00:02:49.040
of the movie to attract potential victims
to take advantage of.

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:52.489
However, while this is a possibility, I don’t
think that’s what happened.

00:02:52.489 --> 00:02:56.400
Paul knows about his son’s sleep issues
and, while I think he will do immoral things

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:59.909
to protect his family, I don’t see him doing
something that would potentially put them

00:02:59.909 --> 00:03:03.769
in danger, especially so soon after having
to put down his own father.

00:03:03.769 --> 00:03:06.900
I believe there is another possibility.

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:11.529
So when they find the dog, he’s lying inside
the house, so he’s out past the red door,

00:03:11.529 --> 00:03:14.049
but not all the way outside past the last
door.

00:03:14.049 --> 00:03:18.549
In simpler terms, if you compare their setup
to an airlock, he’s inside the airlock,

00:03:18.549 --> 00:03:23.189
meaning someone, or something, opened that
door to let the dog in.

00:03:23.189 --> 00:03:27.849
You might argue that the doors were just accidentally
left open, which already seems like an unlikely

00:03:27.849 --> 00:03:32.030
possibility to me given how seriously they
take security, but I think the main factor

00:03:32.030 --> 00:03:34.859
that disproves that is the symptoms of the
dog.

00:03:34.859 --> 00:03:39.819
The human symptoms seem to include large boils,
excessive perspiration and excretion of blood

00:03:39.819 --> 00:03:40.819
from the mouth.

00:03:40.819 --> 00:03:45.549
The dog however looks to have excessive bleeding
and from what I remember, a huge gash in the

00:03:45.549 --> 00:03:50.040
body, and while we don’t know how the symptoms
differ from animals to humans, I would say

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:53.029
it looks more like the dog was attacked than
diseased.

00:03:53.029 --> 00:03:55.620
So then the question becomes, who attacked
the dog?

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:58.879
In the scene where the dog runs away, it’s
basically left open ended.

00:03:58.879 --> 00:04:03.379
He runs off, seems to be barking at something,
goes out of sight, and then suddenly just

00:04:03.379 --> 00:04:04.379
stops barking.

00:04:04.379 --> 00:04:08.650
You could make the case that that’s what
“it” is, some kind of monster or mutant

00:04:08.650 --> 00:04:10.959
that comes out at night to spread the disease.

00:04:10.959 --> 00:04:16.049
And while the idea of that is pretty creepy,
it’s almost less unsettling than the alternative,

00:04:16.049 --> 00:04:20.120
which is that other humans have found out
where they live, come into their house, and

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:22.560
killed their dog to try to spread the disease
to them.

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:26.319
There’s actually more evidence to support
this theory than the monster theory, because

00:04:26.319 --> 00:04:30.320
when Paul and Will go to retrieve Will’s
family, they are attacked by other humans.

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:33.940
For seemingly no reason other than to possibly
jack some supplies.

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:37.979
While we never find out the true intentions
of their aggression thanks in part to Paul’s

00:04:37.979 --> 00:04:41.949
overactive trigger finger, we at least know
that there are people out there in the wilderness

00:04:41.949 --> 00:04:43.690
who want to kill Paul.

00:04:43.690 --> 00:04:47.340
For all we know, they were already on their
way to kill Paul and his family when he and

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:48.669
Will set out.

00:04:48.669 --> 00:04:52.789
So based on all of the information we have,
I would theorize that one of the marauders

00:04:52.789 --> 00:04:56.509
from the wild captured the dog, infected it
and killed it, then dumped it back in the

00:04:56.509 --> 00:05:00.310
house to try and destroy the family, with
the hope of later driving them out and salvaging

00:05:00.310 --> 00:05:01.310
their supplies.

00:05:01.310 --> 00:05:03.040
What Is It?

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:07.970
When I first saw the movie in theaters, it
ended, the credits rolled, and the first thing

00:05:07.970 --> 00:05:15.000
I, or anyone else in the theater said, was,
“so wait, why is it called It Comes At Night?”

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:17.259
Shultsy hasn’t given us an answer on that.

00:05:17.259 --> 00:05:21.311
I’m going to go ahead and assume that he’s
not talking about Stephen King’s IT, or

00:05:21.311 --> 00:05:23.300
the It that Follows people.

00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:27.270
We already talked about the possibility of
there being some kind of monster out there,

00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:30.020
so let’s talk about those possibilities.

00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:33.169
I think the reactions of the dog are the best
evidence for the theory.

00:05:33.169 --> 00:05:37.380
If there is a creature out there, that’s
probably what is causing his animals instincts

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:38.380
to go on high alert.

00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:42.430
It wouldn’t be that different from how animals
seem to have a sense for real predators or

00:05:42.430 --> 00:05:43.930
natural disaster.

00:05:43.930 --> 00:05:48.750
Some say that animals and little kids are
somehow on closer wavelengths to the paranormal,

00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:52.699
and the two who seem to freak out the most
before that fateful night are in fact the

00:05:52.699 --> 00:05:54.270
dog and the child.

00:05:54.270 --> 00:05:58.490
I personally don’t think Shultsy intended
anything paranormal going on though.

00:05:58.490 --> 00:06:01.039
“It” could be more of a metaphorical connotation.

00:06:01.039 --> 00:06:04.490
Could “it” be a reference to the sickness?

00:06:04.490 --> 00:06:08.110
That would seem to make sense, seeing as how
we are never given a proper name for this

00:06:08.110 --> 00:06:09.110
disease.

00:06:09.110 --> 00:06:13.139
And if you think about it, all the characters
who have, or seem to have this illness appear

00:06:13.139 --> 00:06:15.030
to get their symptoms at night.

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:19.599
The grandfather at the beginning of the movie
is put down at night, and the dog shows up

00:06:19.599 --> 00:06:25.509
dying at night, Andrew starts coughing and
crying at night and of course Travis has both

00:06:25.509 --> 00:06:30.130
nightmares about the sickness and the sickness
itself seems to come up at night.

00:06:30.130 --> 00:06:34.599
And while we’re on the topic, another possibility
is that “it” is supposed to represent

00:06:34.599 --> 00:06:38.449
dreams or nightmares, which almost always
come at night.

00:06:38.449 --> 00:06:41.440
Unless you’re me and you stay up making
YouTube videos instead…

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:42.600
I NEED SLEEP.

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:46.991
But if you think about it, dreams kind of
makes sense because all the paranoia, all

00:06:46.991 --> 00:06:51.099
the damage to Travis’s mental health ties
back to his dreams.

00:06:51.099 --> 00:06:54.009
He can’t sleep because his nightmares are
haunted by the sickness.

00:06:54.009 --> 00:06:58.509
His waking hours are all spent trying to strengthen
the family’s protection against the sickness.

00:06:58.509 --> 00:07:03.139
In a way, he has a separate disease of his
own, the one that lives in his head.

00:07:03.139 --> 00:07:07.449
And also, without him being up and about at
night, they probably never catch Will, and

00:07:07.449 --> 00:07:12.240
of course, he probably never contracts the
disease from either Andrew or the dog, whichever

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:14.509
one of the two were the ones to transfer it.

00:07:14.509 --> 00:07:18.770
But above all of that I think that “it”,
is something far scarier.

00:07:18.770 --> 00:07:22.129
Scarier than some monster out in the woods.

00:07:22.129 --> 00:07:26.099
Scarier than some crazy nomads competing for
resources.

00:07:26.099 --> 00:07:29.999
Scarier than a disease that has wiped humanity
to its brink.

00:07:29.999 --> 00:07:34.100
What I’m talking about, is fear itself.

00:07:34.100 --> 00:07:37.870
When you think about it and dig a little bit
deeper, fear and paranoia are what drive all

00:07:37.870 --> 00:07:40.050
the suspense in the entire film.

00:07:40.050 --> 00:07:43.970
When Will first breaks into the house, Travis
and his family fear the unknown intruder.

00:07:43.970 --> 00:07:47.310
When Paul kills the marauders in the wild,
he does so out of fear.

00:07:47.310 --> 00:07:51.660
The two families drift apart and become paranoid
about what they don’t know about each other.

00:07:51.660 --> 00:07:53.729
That’s out of fear.

00:07:53.729 --> 00:07:56.689
That turns them against each other and they
come to fear each other.

00:07:56.689 --> 00:08:00.400
But it’s also important to think about what
builds this fear.

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:04.220
Most of the suspense comes when the families
are locked away in their own rooms, discussing

00:08:04.220 --> 00:08:08.490
their lack of trust for each other because
of what they don’t know about each other’s

00:08:08.490 --> 00:08:09.490
past.

00:08:09.490 --> 00:08:11.560
Discussing what should be done with the other
party.

00:08:11.560 --> 00:08:15.820
Discussing morals and safety precautions all
due to fear of the unknown.

00:08:15.820 --> 00:08:19.930
These conversations, by nature, take place
in private.

00:08:19.930 --> 00:08:21.690
After sun has gone down.

00:08:21.690 --> 00:08:24.259
From the safety of their own bedrooms.

00:08:24.259 --> 00:08:25.949
It comes, at night.

00:08:25.949 --> 00:08:27.220
Water vs Fire

00:08:27.220 --> 00:08:31.699
You may have noticed a couple of opposing
motifs in this movie, and please go ahead

00:08:31.699 --> 00:08:33.570
and let me know which ones you’ve noticed
in the comments.

00:08:33.570 --> 00:08:37.200
I’m going to talk about Water and Fire and
what they symbolize in the film.

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:39.080
Fire and water are opposites.

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:40.500
They cancel each other out.

00:08:40.500 --> 00:08:45.820
However, they are both used for the same purpose
in It Comes At Night: cleansing.

00:08:45.820 --> 00:08:49.310
At the beginning of the film, fire is used
to cleanse the disease from the house when

00:08:49.310 --> 00:08:51.040
they burn Travis’s Grandfather.

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:55.530
The next step for their family is to try to
move on from their loss, and not long after,

00:08:55.530 --> 00:08:57.820
Will shows up looking for supplies.

00:08:57.820 --> 00:09:01.720
Paul captures him and ties him to the tree,
then waits it out to make sure he doesn’t

00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:02.720
have the sickness.

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:05.500
That’s when Paul makes his prisoner an offer.

00:09:05.500 --> 00:09:10.320
He asks him to tell the truth, and in exchange,
he will supply him with water.

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:14.200
So this is the first example of someone using
water to cleanse and achieve purity, even

00:09:14.200 --> 00:09:16.520
if it is less directly than other examples.

00:09:16.520 --> 00:09:20.270
Throughout the film, as Travis struggles with
nightmares of him suffering from the sickness

00:09:20.270 --> 00:09:24.750
or contracting the sickness, he periodically
takes showers to try to not only literally

00:09:24.750 --> 00:09:28.880
keep himself clean and disease free, but also
to try to clear his mind and hopefully help

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:30.530
improve his situation.

00:09:30.530 --> 00:09:34.890
I would also it’s no coincidence that the
shower head is so pathetic and depressing

00:09:34.890 --> 00:09:39.980
and barely manages to keep him covered, similar
to how his current living situation is currently

00:09:39.980 --> 00:09:42.020
barely keeping him covered, keeping him alive.

00:09:42.020 --> 00:09:46.270
We do end up seeing Travis in the bathroom
again, but this time there is no running water

00:09:46.270 --> 00:09:49.670
on him, just a black liquid running from his
nose.

00:09:49.670 --> 00:09:53.620
The precautions that have been taken didn’t
work and now Paul will have to turn back to

00:09:53.620 --> 00:09:56.980
the only cleansing solution that has been
known to work, the fire.

00:09:56.980 --> 00:10:01.260
I’m going to explore this topic a little
deeper in the upcoming segment, so just remember

00:10:01.260 --> 00:10:04.710
what I’ve said about two opposite elements
being used for a similar purpose.

00:10:04.710 --> 00:10:07.460
Who Are The Real Bad Guys?

00:10:07.460 --> 00:10:11.560
Something that It Comes At Night does differently
than a lot of films, is that it remains objective

00:10:11.560 --> 00:10:15.520
about it’s characters and allows the audience
to take sides based on what they see.

00:10:15.520 --> 00:10:20.280
For the most part, we as the viewers are not
manipulated into choosing one side or another,

00:10:20.280 --> 00:10:25.160
except maybe by one fact, which is that we
are introduced to Paul’s family first and

00:10:25.160 --> 00:10:29.760
meet them in the midst of a tragedy, the loss
of a love one, causing us to want to feel

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:31.250
some compassion for them.

00:10:31.250 --> 00:10:35.410
For much of the story, Travis seems to be
our protagonist; his parents: the allies.

00:10:35.410 --> 00:10:39.340
However, before the end of the movie I was
beginning to question Paul and Sarah’s morality

00:10:39.340 --> 00:10:44.400
in the situation, and have come to think that
they may be the real antagonists of the story.

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:49.440
So let’s take a step back and analyse everything
that has happened from an analytical and psychological

00:10:49.440 --> 00:10:50.440
standpoint.

00:10:50.440 --> 00:10:54.630
Paul loses his father to the disease, and
as result becomes extremely strict about the

00:10:54.630 --> 00:10:56.120
family’s routine and safety precautions.

00:10:56.120 --> 00:11:00.130
We don’t really know anything that happens
before this, only that the epidemic didn’t

00:11:00.130 --> 00:11:04.240
take place that long ago because Travis still
remembers what life was like before it.

00:11:04.240 --> 00:11:08.430
Will breaks into the house and Paul shows
no mercy striking him down and taking him

00:11:08.430 --> 00:11:09.430
prisoner.

00:11:09.430 --> 00:11:12.690
At this point I’d like to point that the
story isn’t necessarily told from Paul’s

00:11:12.690 --> 00:11:13.690
point of view.

00:11:13.690 --> 00:11:16.850
Both characters have unknown pasts, which
are occasionally talked about, but it’s

00:11:16.850 --> 00:11:19.670
up to us to figure out if they are lying.

00:11:19.670 --> 00:11:21.120
Well played Shultsy.

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:22.490
Well played.

00:11:22.490 --> 00:11:25.450
Next thing you know, Paul is moving in Will
and his family.

00:11:25.450 --> 00:11:29.860
He doesn’t really give them much of an option,
given his paranoia about having someone out

00:11:29.860 --> 00:11:33.580
there in the world who knows where they are
and knows they have resources.

00:11:33.580 --> 00:11:38.260
So if we can just run with the idea of Will
being the protagonist, then this is his inciting

00:11:38.260 --> 00:11:41.360
incident, this is what kicks the story into
motion for him.

00:11:41.360 --> 00:11:45.380
On the way out, they are both attacked by
marauders, but eventually gain the upper hand.

00:11:45.380 --> 00:11:49.330
Paul finds himself in a position where he
can either let the guy go or kill him.

00:11:49.330 --> 00:11:53.240
Will is thinking logically and suggests that
they try to get some information out of him,

00:11:53.240 --> 00:11:55.480
but Paul just goes ahead and pulls the trigger.

00:11:55.480 --> 00:11:57.860
This shows something about both of their characters.

00:11:57.860 --> 00:12:02.570
This also won’t be the last example of Paul
withholding or avoiding the release of information,

00:12:02.570 --> 00:12:07.270
and as someone who is supposedly a victim,
behaviour as such seems awfully suspicious.

00:12:07.270 --> 00:12:12.560
And then to add to that, when Will questions
him on it, he becomes accusatory, as if he’s

00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:14.810
trying to shift blame off of himself.

00:12:14.810 --> 00:12:16.640
Remember what I said about water and fire?

00:12:16.640 --> 00:12:21.100
Let’s continue to analyse the story, by
framing the characters as these elements.

00:12:21.100 --> 00:12:25.830
Will is water, trying to cleanse the world
and do what’s best for his family in a logical,

00:12:25.830 --> 00:12:27.380
non-destructive way.

00:12:27.380 --> 00:12:33.380
Paul is fire, he’ll burn through anything
to stay clean -- lives, resources, pets, friends

00:12:33.380 --> 00:12:35.320
and even family.

00:12:35.320 --> 00:12:38.890
Let’s fast forward a little bit to the part
where the dog runs off.

00:12:38.890 --> 00:12:40.510
First, Travis gives chase.

00:12:40.510 --> 00:12:44.570
Will takes off after him, and it would seem
his intention is to protect him from whatever

00:12:44.570 --> 00:12:45.570
happens.

00:12:45.570 --> 00:12:49.740
Paul on the other hand, is just FURIOUS at
Travis for endangering himself like that.

00:12:49.740 --> 00:12:53.390
This could be an essential turning point in
the movie, another potential opportunity to

00:12:53.390 --> 00:12:55.130
learn more information.

00:12:55.130 --> 00:12:57.940
Answers to questions like, “What got the
dog so riled up?”

00:12:57.940 --> 00:12:59.580
“What lies beyond their property?”

00:12:59.580 --> 00:13:03.030
“What got to the dog that made it suddenly
stop barking?”

00:13:03.030 --> 00:13:07.750
Paul doesn’t allow any of this to be answered
and uses his dominant position in the household

00:13:07.750 --> 00:13:11.250
to retract both of them and cut off any chance
at learning more.

00:13:11.250 --> 00:13:15.370
Since his goal is to protect his family, he
really could benefit from such information.

00:13:15.370 --> 00:13:19.050
I think this is the point in the story where
Will starts to have second thoughts and realize

00:13:19.050 --> 00:13:23.660
that although Paul may have good intentions
as all good anti-heroes do, he could be going

00:13:23.660 --> 00:13:25.430
about it in a harmful way.

00:13:25.430 --> 00:13:26.710
He’s fire.

00:13:26.710 --> 00:13:27.990
Will’s water.

00:13:27.990 --> 00:13:32.360
This key difference between them continues
to cause clash through the climax of the film.

00:13:32.360 --> 00:13:35.610
Andrew gets sick and they both have different
ways of going about it.

00:13:35.610 --> 00:13:38.520
Will wants to just leave and fend for themselves
out in the wild.

00:13:38.520 --> 00:13:42.070
Paul is uncompromising in his plan to kill
Andrew.

00:13:42.070 --> 00:13:45.770
This part is told from Paul’s point of view
in order to maintain the suspense of the true

00:13:45.770 --> 00:13:47.240
condition Andrew is in.

00:13:47.240 --> 00:13:51.320
Most movies are told through the protagonist,
but I just don’t think that’s the case

00:13:51.320 --> 00:13:52.320
here.

00:13:52.320 --> 00:13:56.200
The guy goes on to kill Will, then as Kim
and Andrew are clearly just terrified and

00:13:56.200 --> 00:13:58.700
trying to escape, he shoots them down as well.

00:13:58.700 --> 00:14:02.560
It’s also heavily implied that he does the
same to his own son.

00:14:02.560 --> 00:14:05.480
I’m not even trying to suggest that he is
entirely in the wrong.

00:14:05.480 --> 00:14:08.990
I think the best villains in fiction have
a good reason for what they are doing, and

00:14:08.990 --> 00:14:11.150
what makes them evil is the way they go about
it.

00:14:11.150 --> 00:14:14.170
I think it’s reasonable to say that Paul
is scarred from what happened to him at the

00:14:14.170 --> 00:14:17.770
beginning of the epidemic, and these scars
have made him jaded and unrelenting.

00:14:17.770 --> 00:14:22.300
I feel like he’s endured something that
anyone in his situation would have the same

00:14:22.300 --> 00:14:23.300
reaction to.

00:14:23.300 --> 00:14:25.320
Did Travis Infect Andrew?

00:14:25.320 --> 00:14:28.860
This theory is going to be a little shorter,
and I don’t necessarily think it’s true,

00:14:28.860 --> 00:14:32.460
but I’d like to bring it up so that we can
open a discussion in the comments.

00:14:32.460 --> 00:14:37.200
WHICH TOTALLY WON’T TURN INTO A SHITSTORM
CAUSE THE COMMENTS ARE SUCH A HAPPY PLA--

00:14:37.200 --> 00:14:41.000
But anyways, what do you guys think about
the idea that when Travis took Andrew’s

00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:45.730
hand to take him back to bed, that he was
infecting the child, not the other way around.

00:14:45.730 --> 00:14:50.130
The only evidence against it is that Andrew
gets his symptoms first, but who knows, maybe

00:14:50.130 --> 00:14:52.430
something like that would show up sooner in
a child.

00:14:52.430 --> 00:14:56.700
I also think it’s possible that Travis has
actually had the sickness for quite a while,

00:14:56.700 --> 00:15:01.050
and it first began to manifest itself as a
psychological attack, which merely precedes

00:15:01.050 --> 00:15:02.460
the physical symptoms.

00:15:02.460 --> 00:15:06.540
That would also explain why we hear Andrew
crying at night after he gets infected before

00:15:06.540 --> 00:15:09.340
we ever see if he actually has the boils or
anything.

00:15:09.340 --> 00:15:11.550
Anyways, just some food for thought.

00:15:11.550 --> 00:15:13.950
Or should I say, pudding bread for thought…?

00:15:13.950 --> 00:15:15.470
What is the disease?

00:15:15.470 --> 00:15:16.990
What does it represent?

00:15:16.990 --> 00:15:21.300
Not much is known about what the disease is
actually supposed to be or what it’s supposed

00:15:21.300 --> 00:15:22.300
to represent.

00:15:22.300 --> 00:15:26.370
We just have a couple of small clues, the
first of which is this painting shown at the

00:15:26.370 --> 00:15:27.370
outset.

00:15:27.370 --> 00:15:31.880
I researched it and found out it is called
The Triumph of Death by Pieter Bruegel.

00:15:31.880 --> 00:15:36.041
Shultsy actually talked about this painting
in an interview with BirthMoviesDeath, link

00:15:36.041 --> 00:15:38.420
in description, where he said the following:

00:15:38.420 --> 00:15:43.170
“So I’d seen the timeless house I wanted
in the film, and I saw the family that should

00:15:43.170 --> 00:15:45.400
live in it, but I wasn’t quite sure why.

00:15:45.400 --> 00:15:50.160
And I had this book on Bruegel, and I stumbled
on The Triumph of Death painting, and found

00:15:50.160 --> 00:15:54.860
out he made it during the Black Death, the
bubonic plague, and I was just haunted by

00:15:54.860 --> 00:15:59.690
it, this vision of skeletons literally pulling
people to their graves in a hellscape.

00:15:59.690 --> 00:16:01.390
I was drawn to it.”

00:16:01.390 --> 00:16:05.980
Shultsy has also mentioned in several interviews
that this movie was essentially binge-written

00:16:05.980 --> 00:16:10.370
at the time of his father’s death, so this
ended up being a very personal piece for him.

00:16:10.370 --> 00:16:14.600
His dad died of cancer after a lot of suffering,
so it would seem that the pain that comes

00:16:14.600 --> 00:16:18.920
to the victim and their family during cancer
treatment inspired the effects of the disease

00:16:18.920 --> 00:16:19.920
in the movie.

00:16:19.920 --> 00:16:23.720
Obviously cancer isn’t contagious, so I
see the fictional disease as a combination

00:16:23.720 --> 00:16:28.170
of the effects of cancer with some of the
symptoms and characteristics of bubonic plague

00:16:28.170 --> 00:16:29.400
in the 14th century.

00:16:29.400 --> 00:16:31.710
Is the ending a dream?

00:16:31.710 --> 00:16:35.600
I was originally going to wrap it up here,
but after surfing around the internet and

00:16:35.600 --> 00:16:40.640
reading some reviews, I decided to cover one
more theory, the idea that the dark ending

00:16:40.640 --> 00:16:45.480
was merely a dream, that the true disease
is actually a mental disease, and Travis is

00:16:45.480 --> 00:16:47.200
being driven crazy by it.

00:16:47.200 --> 00:16:51.760
The theory proposes that his obsessive paranoia
with the disease causes him to lose touch

00:16:51.760 --> 00:16:53.830
of what’s real and what’s not, a la Inception.

00:16:53.830 --> 00:16:54.975
This causes him to fall into a nightmare,
which, by the end of the movie, he is unable

00:16:54.975 --> 00:16:55.975
to pull himself out of.

00:16:55.975 --> 00:17:00.340
The theory is backed on the presentation of
dream scenes in comparison to the climactic

00:17:00.340 --> 00:17:01.340
scene.

00:17:01.340 --> 00:17:04.589
The dreams are all shot in anamorphic lenses,
which basically give the camera a much wider

00:17:04.589 --> 00:17:07.339
angle of view, making characters look smaller
and less significant.

00:17:07.339 --> 00:17:11.280
The music in the climax was also similar to
the dream sequences.

00:17:11.280 --> 00:17:15.370
I like both of those choices, but I just don’t
think Shultsy was trying to say that this

00:17:15.370 --> 00:17:17.030
scene was a literal nightmare.

00:17:17.030 --> 00:17:21.500
I think it was more along the lines of “now
your worst nightmares have become reality.”

00:17:21.500 --> 00:17:24.630
This is what it’s like to live a nightmare.

00:17:24.630 --> 00:17:30.930
I’m basing this off one thing, and one thing
only: the headspace of Trey Edward Shults.

00:17:30.930 --> 00:17:36.050
Writing this movie days after losing his father,
he had to have had a pretty dark outlook on

00:17:36.050 --> 00:17:37.050
life.

00:17:37.050 --> 00:17:39.340
Things probably felt hopeless.

00:17:39.340 --> 00:17:42.030
He probably felt helpless.

00:17:42.030 --> 00:17:47.390
Living in a nightmare, that he knew he couldn’t
wake up from.

00:17:47.390 --> 00:17:50.890
So if you enjoyed this movie, let’s all
show our support to Shultsy.

00:17:50.890 --> 00:17:54.410
Go ahead and like this video if you’d be
interested in seeing him direct another horror

00:17:54.410 --> 00:17:56.880
film and let’s see how things stack up.

00:17:56.880 --> 00:18:02.040
As for me, my name is CZ and I too direct
psychological horror movies among a few other

00:18:02.040 --> 00:18:03.040
things.

00:18:03.040 --> 00:18:06.180
Check out these links to see some of the stuff
I do here on the channel, and if any of it

00:18:06.180 --> 00:18:10.970
looks interesting to you, then go ahead and
subscribe to CZsWorld for new horrors every

00:18:10.970 --> 00:18:11.970
week.

00:18:11.970 --> 00:18:15.400
You’ll also need to ring that deathbell
for notifications and I’ll see you in the

00:18:15.400 --> 00:18:16.410
next one.

00:18:16.410 --> 00:18:18.850
Assuming we both survive.

